ceName": "Atlas Catalan", "verb": "CANONIZES", "targetSlug": "jaume-ferrer", "targetName": "Jaume Ferrer", "context": "The 1375 Atlas Catalan memorialized Jaume Ferrer, depicting his ship and noting his journey 'to find the River of Gold,' thereby preserving his legacy."}], "places": [{"name": "Palma, Spain", "role": "Departure point"}, {"name": "West African Coast", "role": "Destination region"}, {"name": "Cape Bojador, Western Sahara", "role": "Navigational barrier"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Jaume Ferrer", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "jaume-ferrer"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q9011109", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaume_Ferrer"], "tags": ["Explorers & Navigators", "cultural-diffusion", "environmental-history", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Jaume Ferrer's 1346 voyage represents a significant early, albeit mysterious, European attempt to bypass trans-Saharan trade routes for West African gold, directly preceding and influencing later Portuguese exploration. His inscription on the Atlas Catalan solidified his place in cartographic history, signaling the burgeoning era of Atlantic exploration.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-12T08:31:49Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43181064646", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Jaume Ferrer was a Majorcan sailor and explorer. He sailed from Majorca on 10 August 1346 to find the legendary "River of Gold," but the outcome of his quest and his fate are unknown. He is memorialized in his native city of Palma, Majorca.", "newValue": "Jaume Ferrer, a Majorcan mariner of the mid-14th century, embarked on a pivotal but ultimately enigmatic voyage in 1346, seeking the legendary "River of Gold" along the West African coast. His expedition represents an early, albeit ill-fated, precursor to the great European Age of Exploration, driven by burgeoning mercantile ambitions and the lure of fabled African wealth. Operating from the thriving maritime hub of Palma, Majorca, Ferrer's quest underscores the growing European interest in direct access to sub-Saharan resources, circumventing the trans-Saharan trade routes controlled by North African intermediaries.

On 10 August 1346, Ferrer departed from Majorca aboard a galley, with the explicit objective of locating the "River of Gold," a term often associated with the Senegal River or Niger River, renowned for their alluvial gold deposits. Contemporary accounts, notably from the *Atlas Catalan* of 1375 and other portolan charts, depict his likely route past Cape Bojador, a formidable navigational barrier for European sailors of the era. However, beyond his departure and initial course, the precise outcome of Ferrer's journey, the extent of his discoveries, or the circumstances of his eventual fate remain entirely unknown to history. He simply vanished without a trace, leaving no direct record of his return or demise.

